WEBVTT

00:00.600 --> 00:01.622
Module 4.

00:01.972 --> 00:03.992
Prompting for accurate output.

00:04.342 --> 00:07.418
Good prompting does not replace verification, but it sharply

00:07.418 --> 00:10.495
reduces the number of errors you have to catch.

00:10.845 --> 00:14.342
This module gives you a repeatable prompt structure and

00:14.342 --> 00:17.451
techniques that steer AI toward grounded, honest answers.

00:19.201 --> 00:23.303
By the end of this module you will be able to:

00:23.303 --> 00:27.404
use the six-part prompt structure for legal tasks; ground the model

00:27.404 --> 00:31.506
in source material instead of its memory; avoid leading questions that

00:31.506 --> 00:35.234
invite invented support; use decomposition and self-critique to improve accuracy.

00:36.984 --> 00:39.910
Here is a repeatable structure for any legal prompt.

00:40.260 --> 00:43.884
One, role: tell the AI who it is acting as,

00:43.884 --> 00:47.145
for example an associate drafting for a supervising partner.

00:47.495 --> 00:53.393
Two, task: one clear deliverable, such as summarise, compare, draft or list.

00:53.743 --> 00:57.620
Three, jurisdiction and date: which law applies and as

00:57.620 --> 01:01.498
of when, because the model will otherwise blend jurisdictions.

01:01.848 --> 01:06.678
Four, sources: the documents it must rely on, pasted or attached.

01:07.028 --> 01:11.005
Five, constraints: tell it to use only the sources provided, to

01:11.005 --> 01:14.621
say when it is unsure, and never to invent citations.

01:14.971 --> 01:19.400
And six, output format: a table, a numbered list or a memo

01:19.400 --> 01:23.829
with pinpoint references to the source, which makes your verification much faster.

01:25.579 --> 01:27.274
Compare these two prompts.

01:27.624 --> 01:30.547
The weak prompt asks the AI to find

01:30.547 --> 01:33.104
cases that say a non-compete is unenforceable.

01:33.454 --> 01:36.222
It has no jurisdiction, no sources and no

01:36.222 --> 01:38.644
constraints, and it is a leading question.

01:38.994 --> 01:42.523
It practically invites the model to invent supportive authority.

01:42.873 --> 01:46.828
The strong prompt sets a role, gives one task, fixes

01:46.828 --> 01:50.783
the jurisdiction as Texas, supplies three actual decisions, and tells

01:50.783 --> 01:54.739
the model to use only those decisions with pinpoint references.

01:55.088 --> 01:58.681
It asks for points on both sides, tells the model to

01:58.681 --> 02:02.273
say 'not addressed' rather than guess, forbids citing anything not attached,

02:02.273 --> 02:05.212
and asks for a table that makes checking fast.

02:05.562 --> 02:08.427
The strong prompt takes thirty seconds longer to write

02:08.427 --> 02:10.973
and saves far more than that in verification.

02:12.723 --> 02:16.101
The single biggest improvement you can make is to ground the model.

02:16.451 --> 02:18.390
Do not ask it to remember the law.

02:18.740 --> 02:19.785
Give it the law.

02:20.135 --> 02:25.301
Paste or attach the statute, contract, transcript or decision you want analysed.

02:25.651 --> 02:28.745
Require a section, page or paragraph reference for every

02:28.745 --> 02:31.839
point, so each one can be checked in seconds.

02:32.189 --> 02:34.221
And separate finding from analysing.

02:34.571 --> 02:39.029
Use a grounded legal research product, or your usual research database, to

02:39.029 --> 02:43.116
find authority, then use AI to help analyse what you found.

02:43.466 --> 02:46.564
When a general chatbot is asked to find law from

02:46.564 --> 02:49.352
memory, it is working exactly where it is weakest.

02:51.102 --> 02:53.633
Six techniques reliably reduce errors.

02:53.983 --> 02:58.009
Ask for both sides, the strongest authority for and against your

02:58.009 --> 03:01.669
position, which counters the model's tendency to agree with you.

03:02.019 --> 03:06.883
Permit uncertainty explicitly: tell it that 'I don't know' is an acceptable answer.

03:07.233 --> 03:10.507
Break complex work into steps, one per prompt:

03:10.507 --> 03:13.781
facts, then issues, then analysis, then a draft.

03:14.131 --> 03:17.771
Add a self-critique pass, asking the model to list any statement

03:17.771 --> 03:21.411
in its answer that is not supported by the attached sources.

03:21.761 --> 03:24.420
Fix the date the law should be applied as of,

03:24.420 --> 03:27.078
and ask it to flag anything that may have changed.

03:27.428 --> 03:31.480
And ask for gaps: what information would it need to answer reliably?

03:31.830 --> 03:32.817
One caution.

03:33.167 --> 03:37.405
A self-critique pass helps you find problems, but it is never verification.

03:37.755 --> 03:41.330
Asking the AI whether its own answer is correct is not a check.

03:41.680 --> 03:43.724
Only a primary source is.

03:45.474 --> 03:47.900
Finally, match the tool to the task.

03:48.250 --> 03:54.656
AI is a strong fit for summarising documents you provide, first drafts, issue-spotting,

03:54.656 --> 04:00.568
comparing clauses, plain-language explanations for clients, and building chronologies from supplied records.

04:00.918 --> 04:05.855
Use it with care for contract review at scale, research memos built

04:05.855 --> 04:10.380
on authority you supplied, translation, and correspondence to the other side.

04:10.730 --> 04:14.682
And never let AI lead on finding authority from memory,

04:14.682 --> 04:18.633
final citations, deadline and limitation calculations, novel legal questions, or

04:18.633 --> 04:22.190
anything that will be filed without a full check.

04:22.540 --> 04:25.628
The better your prompt, the fewer errors you need to catch.

04:25.978 --> 04:28.288
But good prompting reduces errors.

04:28.638 --> 04:30.960
It never removes the need to verify.

04:32.710 --> 04:34.370
Let's recap the key points.

04:34.720 --> 04:41.663
Use the six parts: role, task, jurisdiction and date, sources, constraints, format.

04:42.013 --> 04:45.891
Ground the model in the documents; don't ask it to remember the law.

04:46.241 --> 04:48.957
Ask for both sides and permit 'I don't know'.

04:49.307 --> 04:52.001
A self-critique pass is not verification.

04:53.751 --> 04:54.865
Knowledge check.

04:55.215 --> 04:58.722
Which prompt is most likely to produce invented authority?

04:59.072 --> 05:04.308
Option A: 'Using only the attached opinion, list each holding with its page number.'.

05:04.658 --> 05:09.720
Option B: 'Find cases supporting my client's position that the contract is void.'.

05:10.070 --> 05:15.143
Option C: 'Summarise the attached contract's termination clauses in a table.'.

05:15.493 --> 05:18.210
Take a moment to decide before the answer appears.

05:23.960 --> 05:25.446
The answer is option B.

05:25.796 --> 05:29.658
A leading request to find supportive cases from memory, with

05:29.658 --> 05:33.133
no sources or constraints, is the classic hallucination trigger.
